<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  MB7 (doc 27 Đợt 6) — Transport security decision, documented honestly:

  This app talks to a *user-configured LAN broker/server* (private RFC1918 IPs
  that differ per factory site, e.g. 192.168.x.x). Android's Network Security
  Config CANNOT express "cleartext allowed only for private IP ranges":
  <domain-config> accepts hostnames / literal IPs only — no CIDR ranges, no
  wildcards over IPs. Because the IP is entered at runtime in Settings, we
  cannot enumerate domains at build time.

  Decision:
   - base-config keeps cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" (functionally the same
     as the old android:usesCleartextTraffic="true", but now explicit and
     documented in one place).
   - TLS IS supported end-to-end already: MQTT via mqtts:// (TCP+SSL) or wss://
     (WebSocket+SSL) through the "useSSL" connection setting, and HTTPS for the
     REST/update endpoints. TLS should be the DEFAULT for new site deployments
     (connection-config UI/default is owned by the settings screen).
   - Note: the raw TCP MQTT path (react-native-tcp-socket) does not consult
     this policy at all — only fetch/WebSocket (OkHttp) do. Setting base-config
     to false would break ws:// LAN MQTT and LAN HTTP APIs while NOT protecting
     the tcp:// path, i.e. pain without gain.

  Tighten-later path (when sites provision TLS or a stable hostname):
   1. Give the LAN server a hostname (mDNS/hosts/DNS) or fixed IP per site.
   2. Flip base-config to cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" and add:
        <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
          <domain includeSubdomains="false">192.168.8.7</domain>
        </domain-config>
      per site (literal IPs are accepted as <domain> entries), or drop the
      exception entirely once TLS is mandatory.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
